{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` is a JSSE engine class representing a secure-socket-protocol implementation. An initialized context supplies `SSLSocketFactory`, `SSLServerSocketFactory`, and `SSLEngine` instances and holds shared state, including session state, for objects created from it. Oracle documents initialization with key managers, trust managers, and a secure-random source.","The finding text, “the observed call sequence is not one SSLContextSpec accepts,” is not a documented `SSLContext` runtime exception or TLS-handshake result. Based on the primary CrySL source, it refers to that rule's event order, forbidden event, literal constraint, and required analysis predicates. The rule source does not contain the presentation label `InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls` and does not identify which individual condition caused a particular diagnostic.","The CrySL source declares `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`; its accepted event order is `Get, Init, Engine?`; `getDefault()` is forbidden; its `getInstance` protocol condition is `protocol in {"TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3"};`; and it requires `generatedKeyManagers[km]`, `generatedTrustManagers[tm]`, and `randomized[random]`.","The requested algorithm name in `SSLContext.getInstance(...)` is not by itself a statement of the enabled or negotiated TLS version. Oracle's standard-names specification says an `SSLContext` implementation requested for a protocol may support other SSL/TLS versions, while enabled protocols are controlled on the created socket, server socket, or engine. Thus, the CrySL rule's literal condition is not a documented runtime determination of which protocol was enabled or negotiated.","Oracle documents obtaining a context using an `SSLContext.getInstance(...)` overload with a supported standard protocol name and, optionally, a provider. A newly obtained context is initialized through `init(KeyManager[] km, TrustManager[] tm, SecureRandom random)` before operations that require initialization. Such operations, including creation of factories or engines, can throw `IllegalStateException` when the context is not initialized.","The official API permits `null` for either manager array in `SSLContext.init`; in that case, installed providers are used to locate the relevant implementation. It also permits a `null` `SecureRandom`, for which a default implementation is used. Therefore, non-null custom arrays and a caller-created random source are not general Java API requirements.","Oracle documents `SSLContext.getDefault()` as returning an immediately usable default context that does not require initialization. This is API-valid behavior, even though the reviewed CrySL rule explicitly forbids `getDefault()`.","For `SSLEngine`, Oracle recommends explicitly setting client or server mode with `setUseClientMode(...)` before other engine operations because the default is provider-specific, and completing connection-specific configuration before the handshake. `SSLParameters` can configure allowed connection protocols, cipher suites, endpoint identification, SNI, client authentication, and related properties. Oracle states that a non-empty endpoint-identification algorithm requires endpoint identification/verification during the handshake.","The reviewed official API materials do not prescribe a CrySL-specific correction. In particular, they do not direct users to replace `"TLS"` with a version literal, avoid `getDefault()`, or replace API-valid null arguments solely to meet this CrySL rule. They document using a provider-supported protocol name and initializing a newly created context before operations that require initialization.","The reviewed `SSLContext` API and JSSE guide do not document an `SSLContext` serialization, persistent/on-disk format, or persisted-format migration path for changes to the requested algorithm string or initialization arguments.","Protocol and enabled-protocol changes can affect peer interoperability during the TLS handshake. Oracle's JSSE guide identifies incompatible peer protocol support as a potential handshake issue and lists `SSLContext.getInstance`, `SSLEngine.setEnabledProtocols`, `SSLSocket.setEnabledProtocols`, and `SSLParameters.setProtocols` as configuration points. The guide says fallback to earlier versions normally reduces security strength and is not suggested unless truly necessary and understood.","For Java SE 25, Oracle documents that every Java-platform implementation must support `TLSv1.2` and `TLSv1.3`; other support is implementation- and release-dependent. The Java SE 11 API documentation instead lists `TLSv1`, `TLSv1.1`, and `TLSv1.2` as required and directs readers to release documentation for other protocols. Compatibility therefore depends on the actual runtime release and provider.","No formal official API migration path was found for converting a generic `"TLS"` request, use of the default context, or null `init` arguments into the particular literal and provenance requirements in this CrySL rule. The official materials distinguish context creation from connection-level protocol configuration.","The exact type declaration in the reviewed CrySL source is `SPEC javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`. The source's matching/event clause, quoted verbatim, is:
```
EVENTS
    g1: getInstance(protocol);
    g2: getInstance(protocol, _);
    Get := g1 | g2;

    i1: init(km, tm, random);
    Init := i1;

    se1: eng = createSSLEngine();
    se2: eng = createSSLEngine(_, _);
    Engine := se1 | se2;
```","The same CrySL source's related rule clauses, quoted verbatim, are:
```
FORBIDDEN
    getDefault() => Get;

ORDER
    Get, Init, Engine?
CONSTRAINTS
    protocol in {"TLSv1.2", "TLSv1.3"};

REQUIRES
    generatedKeyManagers[km];
    generatedTrustManagers[tm];
    randomized[random];
```","The source declares `java.lang.String protocol`, `javax.net.ssl.KeyManager[] km`, `javax.net.ssl.TrustManager[] tm`, `javax.net.ssl.SSLEngine eng`, and `java.security.SecureRandom random`. Its event forms constrain arity and the named declared variables: `getInstance` has one argument or two arguments with an unconstrained second argument; `init` has the three named arguments; `createSSLEngine` has zero arguments or two unconstrained arguments; and `getDefault` has zero arguments.","The rule source does not state that a method merely sharing one of these names on an unrelated type matches the rule. Instead, it declares the governed specification type as `javax.net.ssl.SSLContext` and declares the events within that specification. The source contains no clause extending matching to arbitrary receiver types based only on method name. The source alone does not specify engine-level dispatch, inheritance, aliasing, or unresolved-call matching behavior."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official Java API documentation for the governed type, its factories and engines, initialization semantics, null-argument behavior, default-context behavior, required protocol support in Java SE 25, and engine configuration guidance.","limitations":"Documents Java API behavior, not CrySL detector labels, predicate provenance, or the specific cause of this report.","claim":"Defines `SSLContext` usage and Java SE 25 platform support requirements.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html"},{"relevance":"Official standard-name semantics relevant to distinguishing an `SSLContext.getInstance` algorithm request from enabled or negotiated TLS protocol versions.","limitations":"Does not define CrySL analysis semantics or prescribe a migration to the rule's literal whitelist.","claim":"Documents `SSLContext` algorithm names and that implementations may support additional SSL/TLS versions.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html#sslcontext-algorithms"},{"relevance":"Official guidance on JSSE configuration points, handshake interoperability, and fallback considerations.","limitations":"Does not provide a formal migration path for the CrySL rule or a persisted-format migration.","claim":"Documents that protocol configuration may affect handshake interoperability and identifies relevant configuration APIs.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html"},{"relevance":"Official documentation for `SSLParameters`, including connection-level protocol settings and endpoint-identification behavior.","limitations":"Does not identify the detector condition associated with this finding.","claim":"Documents protocol and endpoint-identification configuration on sockets and engines.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLParameters.html"},{"relevance":"Historical official Java SE 11 API documentation used only for the documented platform-support difference from Java SE 25.","limitations":"Its required-protocol list applies to the Java SE 11 documentation context, not necessarily to another runtime or provider.","claim":"Lists Java SE 11 required SSLContext protocol support and directs readers to release documentation for additional support.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the exact `SSLContextSpec` CrySL type declaration, event patterns, forbidden call, order, literal constraint, and required predicates.","limitations":"The URL is a mutable branch reference. The source does not define the detector UI label, say which condition produced this report, or define all analysis-engine resolution semantics.","claim":"Defines the quoted CrySL rule clauses for `javax.net.ssl.SSLContext`.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL rule source at https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/SSLContext.crysl.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 25 `SSLContext` API documentation at https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 25 SSLContext standard-name documentation at https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html#sslcontext-algorithms.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 25 JSSE Reference Guide at https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-secure-socket-extension-jsse-reference-guide.html.","Reviewed Oracle Java SE 25 `SSLParameters` documentation and Java SE 11 `SSLContext` documentation for the stated compatibility context."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["Which exact rule element caused this particular `InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls` report is unresolved. The rule source does not map that detector presentation label to a specific order violation, forbidden event, failed literal condition, or unsatisfied predicate; raw diagnostic details, detector version, and the compiled rule artifact would be needed.","Whether the mutable `master`-branch source at the cited CrySL URL has exactly the supplied SHA-256 is unresolved from the reviewed material.","The CrySL source does not state how a particular detector resolves dynamic dispatch, inherited methods, aliases, interprocedural values, or statically unknown values. It therefore cannot determine whether a value is treated as unresolved by a specific detector implementation.","The applicable Java runtime release, installed JSSE provider, peer compatibility requirements, and actual enabled-protocol configuration are not supplied. Official sources require those facts to determine platform and interoperability compatibility; they cannot be inferred from an `SSLContext.getInstance(...)` algorithm string alone."]}
